    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d267">

                <pb facs="0860" n="1650" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v01p2/pg1650"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">398.18 ICEM/10–2954</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Director of the Intergovernmental Committee for
                        European Migration (Gibson) to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-10-29">29 October 1954</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: In your personal letter of
                    October 19th 1954<anchor n="1" xml:id="d267-f1"/> you were kind enough to
                    suggest that I might make alternative <pb facs="0861" n="1651" xml:id="pg1651"/>
                    suggestions to the plan put forward by Mr. Warren in his talks with me and his
                    subsequent memorandum dated September 17th.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d267-f2"/> In
                    my memorandum to you dated October 6th,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d267-f3"/> I have
                    requested your official ruling concerning the interpretation of the text of the
                    Appropriations Act which covers the U.S. contribution to the Migration Committee
                    for 1955 (Public Law 778).<anchor n="4" xml:id="d267-f4"/> I have also put
                    forward in that memorandum certain proposals concerning possible action by this
                    Committee. In an additional memorandum which I am attaching to this letter, I am
                    elaborating on these proposals which, in my opinion, would meet the conditions
                    set by the U.S. Congress and might be accepted by our other member governments.
                    I do not believe that they would be prepared to go further. As whatever system
                    is agreed upon will have to be implemented before January 1st, 1955, the matter
                    is urgent. I would therefore welcome as soon as possible your ruling as to
                    interpretation of the “rider” and your reactions to the suggestions contained in
                    the memorandum which is attached to this letter.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hugh Gibson</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d267-a1.xml" xpointer="d267-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
